    {
      "question": "e-8313",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "f0cde272db7b4ea519b42e33f624c57e0cd4237a13d8c1f110fb61be0a64d715",
      "new_sha256": "65424480e11f65caceb8a7c89bde6e4ff09573dd037f16b22397819fdca670a6",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-8313",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we complete the square in the $x$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2x+y & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-2x)+{z}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)^{2}+1+{z}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by rearranging, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x-1)^{2}+1+{z}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-y-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-{z}^{2} & =y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $(1, -1, 0).$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042813/example-8313.json",
          "file_sha256": "54af36f7249cc617fd5b1e52f6cf8f3df74f42fb93de456717b975f517c8cc75"
